  "answer_text": "In American football, the numbers worn by linebackers have changed significantly in recent years due to major rule updates. \n\nCurrently, linebacker numbers are divided between **traditional classics** and **modern single-digit/skill-position styles**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Modern NFL Jersey Rules (Current)\nUnder current NFL rules, linebackers have immense flexibility and are allowed to wear:\n* **0\u201359**\n* **90\u201399**\n\n#### The \"New School\" Style (Single Digits and Teens)\nFollowing a landmark rule change in 2021 (and the addition of the number 0 in 2023), many modern NFL linebackers have ditched traditional blocky numbers in favor of single-digits and teens. This style is highly popular because it mirrors what many players wore in college.\n* **Examples:** \n  * **Roquan Smith** (#0)\n  * **Micah Parsons** (#11)\n  * **Patrick Queen** (#6)\n  * **Nick Bolton** (#32)\n\n---\n\n### 2. Traditional NFL Linebacker Numbers (The Classics)\nHistorically, the NFL was very strict about positioning numbers. For decades, linebackers were defined by two main digit ranges:\n\n#### **The 50s (Numbers 50\u201359)**\nHistorically, this is the most iconic linebacker range, especially for middle and inside linebackers (ILBs/MLBs). It projects toughness, bulk, and leadership. \n* **50:** Mike Singletary\n* **51:** Dick Butkus\n* **52:** Ray Lewis, Patrick Willis, Khalil Mack\n* **54:** Brian Urlacher, Bobby Wagner, Fred Warner\n* **55:** Junior Seau, Derrick Brooks\n* **56:** Lawrence Taylor, Derrick Thomas\n* **59:** Luke Kuechly\n\n#### **The 90s (Numbers 90\u201399)**\nIn 1984, the NFL opened the 90s to linebackers to accommodate the rise of the 3-4 defense. Today, this range is mostly worn by **outside linebackers (OLBs) and edge rushers** who split their identities between linebackers and defensive linemen.\n* **90:** T.J. Watt\n* **91:** Ryan Kerrigan\n* **97:** Joey Bosa\n\n#### **The 40s (Numbers 40\u201349)**\nThe NFL allowed linebackers to wear the 40s starting in 2015 to ease number shortages. \n* **40:** Von Miller (during his time with the Rams and Bills)\n* **45:** Devin White (during his time with the Buccaneers)\n\n---\n\n### 3. College Football (NCAA) Rules\nIn college football, numbering is much less restrictive. Linebackers can wear **any number from 0 to 99**. \n* It is incredibly common for star college linebackers to wear highly-coveted **single-digit numbers (1\u20139)**, which are often handed out by coaches as badges of honor to the team's best defensive players.",
